   CodeDivisionBench(; name, bit_time, __overrides)

Code-division multiplexing: two bit streams share one wire at the same time and in the same band.

Each sender multiplies its bits (+1 or -1, one per `bit_time`) by its own spreading code of four
chips per bit, and the two spread signals add onto the wire. The codes are rows of the 4 x 4
Walsh matrix, which are orthogonal: code A is + - + - (a square wave at 2 / `bit_time`), code B
is + + - - (a square wave at 1 / `bit_time`). The wire takes the values -2, 0 and +2 and looks
like noise. Each receiver multiplies the wire by its own code and averages over the bit: its own
bits come back as +1 or -1, and the other sender's product averages to zero. A third receiver
uses code C = A x B = + - - +, which nobody sent, and receives zero.

## Parameters:

| Name         | Description                         | Units  |   Default value |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------- | ------ | --------------- |
| `bit_time`         | Duration of one bit; a code has four chips per bit                         | s  |   1.0 |
